    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "yeah... the lack of resistance. that part resonates. i was thinking about how he deals with that feeling though. right now, he's just staring at the wall, and he feels like there's no point in even trying to fix anything because why bother? it's like he's just waiting for something to happen, but he knows nothing will. how do i make that feel real for the reader? how do i show that he's reached that point where he just... can't do it anymore?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Don't make him *decide* to stop; let the inertia take over. Show him noticing the small, pointless things—the dust motes dancing, the slow drip of a faucet—and feeling absolutely nothing about them. The lack of resistance becomes a quiet, perfect surrender, like sinking into a too-soft mattress and realizing you don't want to get up. That stillness, that absolute refusal to move, is where the real weight lives.",
      "source": "target",
